24::
0:
Program
1:
Argument error\n
Task %.16s: More than one\n
occurence of optional parameter\n
%.16s\n
\t\n
Make sure that the optional parameter is\n
not specified more than once in the same\n
routine call.
2:
Argument error\n
Task %.16s: Excluding\n
arguments must have conditional value\n
(%.16s has value)\n
\t\n
Arguments may not be specified for more\n
than one parameter from a list of\n
parameters that exclude each other\n
unless all values are conditional\n
argument values.
3:
Argument error\n
Task %.16s: Expecting\n
argument for required parameter\n
%.16s but found optional\n
argument %.16s\n
\t\n
Check that the arguments are specified\n
in the same order as the parameters for\n
the routine being called.
4:
Argument error\n
Task %.16s: Argument for REF\n
parameter %.16s is not data\n
reference\n
\t\n
Make sure the argument expression is\n
just a data or parameter reference.
5:
Argument error\n
Task %.16s: Argument for\n
'INOUT' parameter %.16s is\n
not variable or persistent reference or\n
is read only\n
\t\n
Make sure the argument expression is\n
just a variable, persistent, variable\n
parameter or persistent parameter\n
reference. The variable or persistent\n
may not be read only.
6:
Argument error\n
Task %.16s: Missing optional\n
argument value for parameter\n
%.16s\n
\t\n
Only 'switch' parameters may be\n
specified by name only. Optional\n
parameters of other types must be\n
assigned a value. Add a value.
7:
Argument error\n
Task %.16s: Optional argument\n
%.16s at wrong place in\n
argument list\n
\t\n
Check that the arguments are specified\n
in the same order as the parameters for\n
the routine being called.
8:
Argument error\n
Task %.16s: Reference to\n
optional parameter %.16s in\n
required argument\n
\t\n
An argument corresponding to an optional\n
parameter must be specified with a\n
leading '\' character. Change the\n
required argument into an optional.
9:
Argument error\n
Task %.16s: Reference to\n
required parameter %.16s in\n
conditional argument value\n
\t\n
A conditional value for an optional\n
parameter must refer an optional\n
parameter in the calling routine.\n
Change the conditional value.
10:
Argument error\n
Task %.16s: Reference to\n
required parameter %.16s in\n
optional argument\n
\t\n
An argument corresponding to a required\n
parameter must not be specified with the\n
leading '\' character. Change the\n
optional argument into a reguired.
11:
Argument error\n
Task %.16s: Named required\n
argument %.16s at wrong place\n
in argument list\n
\t\n
Check that the arguments are specified\n
in the same order as the parameters for\n
the routine being called.
12:
Argument error\n
Task %.16s: 'switch' argument\n
%.16s cannot have a value\n
\t\n
An argument corresponding to a 'switch'\n
parameter may not be assigned a value.\n
Remove the value.
13:
Argument error\n
Task %.16s: Too few arguments\n
in call to routine %.16s\n
\t\n
A routine call must supply values for\n
all required parameters of the routine\n
being called. Add more arguments to fit\n
the parameter list.
14:
Argument error\n
Task %.16s: Too many\n
arguments in call to routine\n
%.16s\n
\t\n
Remove arguments so that no arguments\n
are supplied in excess to those defined\n
by the parameter list of the called\n
routine.
15:
Data declaration error\n
Task %.16s: Array dimension\n
must be > 0 (value is %i)\n
\t\n
Array dimensions must be positive.\n
Change the dimension expression.
16:
Data declaration error\n
Task %.16s: Too many\n
dimensions in array definition\n
\t\n
An array may have at most 3 dimensions.\n
Rewrite the program so that no more than\n
3 dimensions are needed.
17:
Type error\n
Task %.16s: Indexed data\n
%.18s %.18s is\n
not of array type\n
\t\n
Only data that have been declared to be\n
arrays may be indexed. Remove the index\n
or indices, or declare the data to be an\n
array.
18:
Type error\n
Task %.16s: Data\n
%.18s %.18s is\n
not of record type\n
\t\n
Components are only available for data\n
of record type. Check the type and name\n
of the referenced data.
19:
Limit error\n
Task %.16s: Error %i when\n
creating sdb entry for\n
%.16s\n
\t\n
An error occurred when the persistent\n
was to be inserted into the shared\n
database. Probably the database is full.
20:
Data declaration error\n
Task %.16s: Expression not\n
constant expression (%.16s\n
not constant)\n
\t\n
Expressions contained within data\n
declarations must be constant\n
expressions. Make sure the expression\n
does not contain any variable or\n
persistent reference, or function call.
21:
Instruction error\n
Task %.16s: RETURN from\n
function must have an expression\n
\t\n
A RETURN instruction within a function\n
must specify a function value to be\n
returned. Add a value expression.
22:
Type error\n
Task %.16s: Illegal\n
combination of operand types\n
%.18s and\n
%.18s for '*' operator\n
\t\n
The allowed type combinations for the\n
two operands of the '*' operator are\n
'num'*'num', 'num'*'pos', 'pos'*'num',\n
'pos'*'pos' and 'orient'*'orient'. Check\n
the types of the operands.
23:
Instruction error\n
Task %.16s: Cannot transfer\n
control into another instruction list\n
\t\n
Make sure that the label is located in\n
the same instruction list as the GOTO\n
instruction, at the same or an outer\n
level. It is not possible to jump into\n
a program flow instruction.
24:
Type error\n
Task %.16s: Illegal type\n
%.18s for left operand of\n
binary '+' or '-' operator\n
\t\n
The allowed types for the operands of\n
the '+' operator are 'num', 'pos' and\n
'string', for the '-' operator 'num' and\n
'pos'. Check the type of the operand.
25:
Type error\n
Task %.16s: Illegal type\n
%.18s for operand of unary\n
'+' or '-' operator\n
\t\n
The allowed types for the operands of\n
the '+' and '-' operators are 'num' and\n
'pos'. Check the type of the operand.
26:
Type error\n
Task %.16s: Illegal type\n
%.18s for right operand of\n
binary '+' or '-' operator\n
\t\n
The allowed types for the operands of\n
the '+' operator are 'num', 'pos' and\n
'string', for the '-' operator 'num' and\n
'pos'. Check the type of the operand.
27:
Type error\n
Task %.16s: Illegal type\n
%.18s for left operand of\n
'/', 'DIV' or 'MOD' operator\n
\t\n
The only allowed type for the operands\n
of the '/', 'DIV' and 'MOD' operators\n
is 'num'. Check the type of the operand.
28:
Type error\n
Task %.16s: Illegal type\n
%.18s for right operand of\n
'/', 'DIV' or 'MOD' operator\n
\t\n
The only allowed type for the operands\n
of the '/', 'DIV' and 'MOD' operators\n
is 'num'. Check the type of the operand.
29:
Type error\n
Task %.16s: Illegal type\n
%.18s for left operand of\n
'<', '<=', '>' or '>=' operator\n
\t\n
The only allowed type for the operands\n
of the '<', '<=', '>' and '>=' operators\n
is 'num'. Check the type of the operand.
30:
Type error\n
Task %.16s: Illegal type\n
%.18s for right operand of\n
'<', '<=', '>' or '>=' operator\n
\t\n
The only allowed type for the operands\n
of the '<', '<=', '>' and '>=' operators\n
is 'num'. Check the type of the operand.
31:
Type error\n
Task %.16s: Illegal type\n
%.18s for left operand of\n
'*' operator\n
\t\n
The allowed types for the operands of\n
the '*' operator are 'num', 'pos' and\n
'orient'. Check the type of the operand.
32:
Type error\n
Task %.16s: Illegal type\n
%.18s for right operand of\n
'*' operator\n
\t\n
The allowed types for the operands of\n
the '*' operator are 'num', 'pos' and\n
'orient'. Check the type of the operand.
33:
Type error\n
Task %.16s: Illegal type\n
%.18s for operand of 'NOT'\n
operator\n
\t\n
The only allowed type for the operand of\n
the 'NOT' operator is 'bool'. Check the\n
type of the operand.
34:
Type error\n
Task %.16s: Illegal type\n
%.18s for left operand of\n
'OR', 'XOR' or 'AND' operator\n
\t\n
The only allowed type for the operands\n
of the 'OR', 'XOR' and "AND' operator is\n
'bool'. Check the type of the operand.
35:
Type error\n
Task %.16s: Illegal type\n
%.18s for right operand of\n
'OR', 'XOR' or 'AND' operator\n
\t\n
The only allowed type for the operands\n
of the 'OR', 'XOR' and "AND' operator is\n
'bool'. Check the type of the operand.
36:
Type error\n
Task %.16s: Incorrect number\n
of indices in index list for array\n
%.18s with %i dimension(s)\n
\t\n
Make sure that the number of indices in\n
the index list is the same as the number\n
of dimensions of the indexed data array.
37:
Data declaration error\n
Task %.16s: LOCAL illegal in\n
routine constant declaration\n
\t\n
Only program data declarations may have\n
the LOCAL attribute. Remove the LOCAL\n
attribute or move the declaration\n
outside of the routine.
38:
Data declaration error\n
Task %.16s: LOCAL illegal in\n
routine variable declaration\n
\t\n
Only program data declarations may have\n
the LOCAL attribute. Remove the LOCAL\n
attribute or move the declaration\n
outside of the routine.
39:
Name error\n
Task %.16s: Constant name\n
%.16s ambiguous\n
\t\n
Routine data must have names that are\n
unique within the routine. Program data\n
must have names that are unique within\n
the module. Rename the data or change\n
the conflicting name.
40:
Name error\n
Task %.16s: Global constant\n
name %.16s ambiguous\n
\t\n
Global data must have names that are\n
unique among all the global types, data,\n
global routines and modules in the\n
entire program. Rename the data\n
or change the conflicting name.
41:
Name error\n
Task %.16s: Global persistent\n
name %.16s ambiguous\n
\t\n
Global data must have names that are\n
unique among all the global types, data,\n
global routines and modules in the\n
entire program. Rename the data\n
or change the conflicting name.
42:
Name error\n
Task %.16s: Global routine\n
name %.16s ambiguous\n
\t\n
Global routines must have names that are\n
unique among all the global types, data,\n
global routines and modules in the\n
entire program. Rename the routine\n
or change the conflicting name.
43:
Name error\n
Task %.16s: Global variable\n
name %.16s ambiguous\n
\t\n
Global data must have names that are\n
unique among all the global types, data,\n
global routines and modules in the\n
entire program. Rename the data\n
or change the conflicting name.
44:
Name error\n
Task %.16s: Label name\n
%.16s ambiguous\n
\t\n
Labels must have names that are unique\n
within the routine. Rename the label or\n
change the conflicting name.
45:
Name error\n
Task %.16s: Module name\n
%.16s ambiguous\n
\t\n
Modules must have names that are unique\n
among all the global types, global data,\n
global routines and modules in the\n
entire program. Rename the module\n
or change the conflicting name.
46:
Name error\n
Task %.16s: Parameter name\n
%.16s ambiguous\n
\t\n
Parameters must have names that are\n
unique within the routine. Rename the\n
parameter or change the conflicting\n
name.
47:
Name error\n
Task %.16s: Persistent name\n
%.16s ambiguous\n
\t\n
Program data must have names that are\n
unique within the module. Rename the\n
data or change the conflicting name.
48:
Name error\n
Task %.16s: Routine name\n
%.16s ambiguous\n
\t\n
Routines must have names that are unique\n
within the module. Rename the routine or\n
change the conflicting name.
49:
Name error\n
Task %.16s: Variable name\n
%.16s ambiguous\n
\t\n
Routine data must have names that are\n
unique within the routine. Program data\n
must have names that are unique within\n
the module. Rename the data or change\n
the conflicting name.
50:
Type error\n
Task %.16s: Operand types\n
%.18s and\n
%.18s for binary '+' or '-'\n
operator not equal\n
\t\n
The two operands of the '+' and '-'\n
operators must have equal type. Check\n
the operand types.
51:
Type error\n
Task %.16s: Operand types\n
%.18s and\n
%.18s for '=' or '<>'\n
operator not equal\n
\t\n
The two operands of the '=' and '<>'\n
operators must have equal type. Check\n
the operand types.
52:
Instruction error\n
Task %.16s: RETURN with\n
expression only allowed in function\n
\t\n
In a procedure or trap the RETURN\n
instruction must not specify a return\n
value expression. Remove the expression.
53:
Instruction error\n
Task %.16s: RAISE in error\n
handler must not have an expression\n
\t\n
A RAISE instruction within an error\n
handler can only be used to propagate\n
the current error, and may therefore not\n
specify an error number. Remove the\n
error number expression.
54:
Type error\n
Task %.16s: Different\n
dimension of array type (%i) and\n
aggregate (%i)\n
\t\n
Make sure that the number of expressions\n
in the aggregate is the same as the\n
dimension of the data array.
55:
Type error\n
Task %.16s: Assignment target\n
type %.18s is not value or\n
semi-value type\n
\t\n
The type, of the data to be assigned a\n
value, must be a value or semi-value\n
type. Data of non-value types may only\n
be set by special type specific\n
predefined instructions or functions.
56:
Type error\n
Task %.16s: Type\n
%.18s for left operand of\n
'=' or '<>' operator not value or\n
semi-value type\n
\t\n
The '=' and '<>' operators may only be\n
applied to expressions of value or semi-\n
value type. If comparisons are to be\n
made, special type specific predefined\n
functions are needed.
57:
Type error\n
Task %.16s: Type\n
%.18s for right operand of\n
'=' or '<>' operator not value or\n
semi-value type\n
\t\n
The '=' and '<>' operators may only be\n
applied to expressions of value or semi-\n
value type. If comparisons are to be\n
made, special type specific predefined\n
functions are needed.
58:
Type error\n
Task %.16s: TEST expression\n
type %.18s not value or\n
semi-value type\n
\t\n
The TEST instruction may only be applied\n
to an expression of value or semi-value\n
type. If comparisons are to be made,\n
special type specific predefined\n
functions are needed.
59:
Data declaration error\n
Task %.16s: Place holder for\n
value expression not allowed in\n
definition of named constant\n
\t\n
Complete the data declaration or change\n
the data name to a place holder.
60:
Data declaration error\n
Task %.16s: Place holder for\n
array dimension not allowed in\n
definition of named constant or variable\n
\t\n
Complete the data declaration or change\n
the data name to a place holder.
61:
Routine declaration error\n
Task %.16s: Place holder for\n
parameter array dimensions not allowed\n
in definition of named routine\n
\t\n
Complete the parameter declaration or\n
change the routine name to a place\n
holder.
62:
Name error\n
Task %.16s: Place holder for\n
parameter name not allowed in definition\n
of named routine\n
\t\n
Complete the routine declaration or\n
change the routine name to a place\n
holder.
63:
Data declaration error\n
Task %.16s: Place holder for\n
initial value expression not allowed in\n
definition of named persistent\n
\t\n
Complete the data declaration or change\n
the data name to a place holder.
64:
Routine declaration error\n
Task %.16s: Place holder for\n
parameter not allowed in definition of\n
named routine\n
\t\n
Complete the parameter declaration,\n
remove the place holder or change the\n
routine name to a place holder.
65:
Reference error\n
Task %.16s: Place holder for\n
type not allowed in definition of named\n
data, record component or routine\n
\t\n
Complete the data or routine declaration\n
or change the data or routine name to a\n
place holder.
66:
Data declaration error\n
Task %.16s: Place holder for\n
initial value expression not allowed in\n
definition of named variable\n
\t\n
Complete the data declaration or change\n
the data name to a place holder.
67:
Type error\n
Task %.16s: Too few\n
components in record aggregate of type\n
%.18s\n
\t\n
Make sure that the number of expressions\n
in the aggregate is the same as the\n
number of components in the record type.
68:
Type error\n
Task %.16s: Too many\n
components in record aggregate of type\n
%.18s\n
\t\n
Make sure that the number of expressions\n
in the aggregate is the same as the\n
number of components in the record type.
69:
Reference error\n
Task %.16s: Data reference\n
%.16s is ambiguous\n
\t\n
At least one other object sharing the\n
same name as the referred data is\n
visible from this program position. Make\n
sure that all object names fulfill the\n
naming rules regarding uniqueness.
70:
Reference error\n
Task %.16s: Function\n
reference %.16s is ambiguous\n
\t\n
At least one other object sharing the\n
same name as the referred function is\n
visible from this program position. Make\n
sure that all object names fulfill the\n
naming rules regarding uniqueness.
71:
Reference error\n
Task %.16s: Label reference\n
%.16s is ambiguous\n
\t\n
At least one other object sharing the\n
same name as the referred label is\n
visible from this program position. Make\n
sure that all object names fulfill the\n
naming rules regarding uniqueness.
72:
Reference error\n
Task %.16s: Procedure\n
reference %.16s is ambiguous\n
\t\n
At least one other object sharing the\n
same name as the referred procedure is\n
visible from this program position. Make\n
sure that all object names fulfill the\n
naming rules regarding uniqueness.
73:
Reference error\n
Task %.16s: Trap reference\n
%.16s is ambiguous\n
\t\n
At least one other object sharing the\n
same name as the referred trap is\n
visible from this program position. Make\n
sure that all object names fulfill the\n
naming rules regarding uniqueness.
74:
Reference error\n
Task %.16s: %.16s\n
not entire data reference\n
\t\n
The specified name identifies an object\n
other than data. Check if the desired\n
data is hidden by some other object with\n
the same name.
75:
Reference error\n
Task %.16s: %.16s\n
not function reference\n
\t\n
The specified name identifies an object\n
other than a function. Check if the\n
desired function is hidden by some other\n
object with the same name.
76:
Reference error\n
Task %.16s: %.16s\n
not label reference\n
\t\n
The specified name identifies an object\n
other than a label. Check if the desired\n
label is hidden by some other object\n
with the same name.
77:
Reference error\n
Task %.16s: %.16s\n
not optional parameter reference in\n
conditional argument value\n
\t\n
The specified name identifies an object\n
other than an optional parameter. Change\n
the name to refer to an optional\n
parameter.
78:
Reference error\n
Task %.16s: %.16s\n
not optional parameter reference\n
\t\n
The specified name identifies an object\n
other than an optional parameter. Change\n
the name to refer to an optional\n
parameter.
79:
Reference error\n
Task %.16s: %.16s\n
not procedure reference\n
\t\n
The specified name identifies an object\n
other than a procedure. Check if the\n
desired procedure is hidden by some\n
other object with the same name.
80:
Reference error\n
Task %.16s: %.16s\n
not required parameter reference\n
\t\n
The specified name identifies an object\n
other than a required parameter. Change\n
the name to refer to a required\n
parameter.
81:
Reference error\n
Task %.16s: %.16s\n
not trap reference\n
\t\n
The specified name identifies an object\n
other than a trap. Check if the desired\n
trap is hidden by some other object with\n
the same name.
82:
Reference error\n
Task %.16s: %.16s\n
not type name\n
\t\n
The specified name identifies an object\n
other than a type. Check if the desired\n
type is hidden by some other object with\n
the same name.
83:
Type error\n
Task %.16s: %.16s\n
not value type\n
\t\n
Only variables which lack initial value,\n
and 'VAR' mode parameters may be of\n
semi-value or non-value type.
86:
Reference error\n
Task %.16s: Reference to\n
unknown label %.16s\n
\t\n
The routine contains no label (or other\n
object) with the specified name.
87:
Reference error\n
Task %.16s: Reference to\n
unknown optional parameter\n
%.16s\n
\t\n
The called routine contains no optional\n
parameter (or other object) with the\n
specified name.
89:
Reference error\n
Task %.16s: Reference to\n
unknown record component\n
%.16s\n
\t\n
The record type contains no record\n
component with the specified name.
90:
Reference error\n
Task %.16s: Reference to\n
unknown required parameter\n
%.16s\n
\t\n
The called routine contains no required\n
parameter (or other object) with the\n
specified name.
92:
Reference error\n
Task %.16s: Unknown type name\n
%.16s\n
\t\n
No data type (or other object) with the\n
specified name is visible from this\n
program position.
93:
Instruction error\n
Task %.16s: Assignment target\n
is read only\n
\t\n
The data to be assigned a value may not\n
be a constant, read only variable or\n
read only persistent.
94:
Data declaration error\n
Task %.16s: Persistent\n
declaration not allowed in routine\n
\t\n
Persistents may only be declared at\n
module level. Move the persistent\n
declaration from the routine.
95:
Instruction error\n
Task %.16s: RAISE without\n
expression only allowed in error handler\n
\t\n
Add an error number expression to the\n
RAISE instruction.
96:
Instruction error\n
Task %.16s: RETRY only\n
allowed in error handler\n
\t\n
The RETRY instruction may only be used\n
in error handlers. Remove it.
97:
Instruction error\n
Task %.16s: TRYNEXT only\n
allowed in error handler\n
\t\n
The TRYNEXT instruction may only be used\n
in error handlers. Remove it.
98:
Parameter error\n
Task %.16s: 'switch'\n
parameter must have transfer mode IN\n
\t\n
Remove the parameter transfer mode\n
specifier. If IN transfer mode is not\n
sufficient, change the data type of the\n
parameter.
99:
Parameter error\n
Task %.16s: 'switch'\n
parameter cannot be dimensioned\n
\t\n
Remove the array dimension\n
specification, or change the data type\n
of the parameter.
100:
Parameter error\n
Task %.16s: 'switch' only\n
allowed for optional parameter\n
\t\n
Change the parameter into an optional\n
parameter, or change the data type of\n
the parameter. If the object is not a\n
parameter, change the data type.
101:
Type error\n
Task %.16s: Type mismatch of\n
exptected type %.18s and\n
type %.18s\n
\t\n
The expression is not of the expected\n
data type.
102:
Type error\n
Task %.16s: Type mismatch of\n
aggregate, expected type\n
%.18s\n
\t\n
The aggregate does not match the\n
expected data type.
103:
Type error\n
Task %.16s: Persistent\n
%.18s %.16s type\n
mismatch\n
\t\n
There is already a persistent data with\n
the same name but with another data\n
type. Rename the persistent, or change\n
its data type.
104:
Data declaration error\n
Task %.16s: Cannot determine\n
array dimensions (circular constant\n
references ?)\n
\t\n
Check that any referred constants are\n
correctly defined. If so, the program is\n
too complex. Try to rewrite the\n
declarations.
105:
Data declaration error\n
Task %.16s: Cannot determine\n
type of constant value (circular\n
constant references ?)\n
\t\n
Check that any referred constants are\n
correctly defined. If so, the program is\n
too complex. Try to rewrite the\n
declarations.
106:
Data declaration error\n
Task %.16s: Cannot evaluate\n
constant value expression (circular\n
constant references ?)\n
\t\n
Check that any referred constants are\n
correctly defined. If so, the program is\n
too complex. Try to rewrite the\n
declarations.
107:
Data declaration error\n
Task %.16s: Cannot determine\n
type of variable value (circular\n
constant references?)\n
\t\n
Check that any referred constants are\n
correctly defined. If so, the program is\n
too complex. Try to rewrite the\n
declarations.
108:
Type error\n
Task %.16s: Unknown aggregate\n
type\n
\t\n
An aggregate may not be used in this\n
position since there is no expected data\n
type. Declare data with the desired data\n
type and aggregate value. Use the name\n
of the data instead of the aggregate.
109:
Type definition error\n
Task %.16s: Cannot determine\n
type of record component\n
%.16s\n
(circular type definitions?)\n
\t\n
Check that the type of the component is\n
correctly defined. If so, it could be\n
a circular definition, the type of a\n
component could not refere to the its\n
own record type.
110:
Reference error\n
Task %.16s: Record name\n
%.16s is ambiguous\n
\t\n
At least one other object sharing the\n
same name as the referred record name is\n
visible from this program position. Make\n
sure that all object names fulfill the\n
naming rules regarding uniqueness.
111:
Name error\n
Task %.16s: Global record\n
name %.16s ambiguous\n
\t\n
Global type must have names that are\n
unique among all the global types, data,\n
global routines and modules in the\n
entire program. Rename the record\n
or change the conflicting name.
112:
Reference error\n
Task %.16s: Alias name\n
%.16s is ambiguous\n
\t\n
At least one other object sharing the\n
same name as the referred alias name is\n
visible from this program position. Make\n
sure that all object names fulfill the\n
naming rules regarding uniqueness.
113:
Name error\n
Task %.16s: Global alias\n
name %.16s ambiguous\n
\t\n
Global type must have names that are\n
unique among all the global types, data,\n
global routines and modules in the\n
entire program. Rename the alias\n
or change the conflicting name.
114:
Type definition error\n
Task %.16s: Type reference\n
of alias name %.16s\n
is an alias type\n
\t\n
Check that the type of the component is\n
correctly defined. If so, it could be\n
a circular definition, the type of a\n
component could not refere to the its\n
own record type.
115:
Type definition error\n
Task %.16s: Cannot determine\n
type of alias %.16s\n
(circular type definitions?)\n
\t\n
Check that the type of the alias is\n
correctly defined. If so, it could be\n
a circular definition, the type of an\n
alias could not refere to a record that\n
use this alias as a component.
116:
Reference error\n
Task %.16s:\n
Record component name\n
%.16s is ambiguous\n
\t\n
At least one other object sharing the\n
same name as the referred component is\n
visible from this program position. Make\n
sure that all object names fulfill the\n
naming rules regarding uniqueness.
117:
Type definition error\n
Task %.16s: Place holder for\n
record component not allowed in\n
definition of named record\n
\t\n
Complete the definition or change\n
the data name to a place holder.
118:
Not authorized\n
Task %.16s: The Option\n
User defined data types is not\n
installed in this system\n
\t\n

141:
Argument error\n
Task %.16s: Argument for\n
'PERS' parameter %.16s is not\n
persistent reference or is read only\n
\t\n
Make sure the argument expression is\n
just a persistent or persistent\n
parameter reference. The persistent may\n
not be read only.
142:
Argument error\n
Task %.16s: Argument for\n
'VAR' parameter %.16s is not\n
variable reference or is read only\n
\t\n
Make sure the argument expression is\n
just a variable or variable parameter\n
reference. The variable may not be read\n
only.
157:
Instruction error\n
Task %.16s: Interrupt number\n
is not static variable reference or is\n
read only\n
\t\n
Make sure the interrupt number is just\n
a variable or variable parameter\n
reference. The variable must be\n
static. The variable may not be read\n
only.
158:
Value error\n
Task %.16s: Integer value\n
%G too large\n
\t\n
The value of the expression must be an\n
integer value. The current value is\n
outside the integer range.
159:
Value error\n
Task %.16s: %G not\n
integer value\n
\t\n
The value of the expression must be an\n
exact integer value. The current value\n
has a fraction part.
165:
Reference error\n
Task %.16s: Reference to\n
unknown entire data %.16s\n
\t\n
No data (or other object) with the\n
specified name is visible from this\n
program position.
166:
Reference error\n
Task %.16s: Reference to\n
unknown function %.16s\n
\t\n
No function (or other object) with the\n
specified name is visible from this\n
program position.
168:
Reference error\n
Task %.16s: Reference to\n
unknown procedure %.16s\n
\t\n
No procedure (or other object) with the\n
specified name is visible from this\n
program position.
170:
Reference error\n
Task %.16s: Reference to\n
unknown trap %.16s\n
\t\n
No trap (or other object) with the\n
specified name is visible from this\n
program position.
191:
Instruction error\n
Task %.16s: Variable and trap\n
routine already connected\n
\t\n
It is not legal to connect a specific\n
variable with a specific trap routine\n
more than once.
192:
Argument error\n
Task %.16s: %.16s\n
is second present conditional argument\n
for excluding parameters\n
\t\n
Arguments may not be present for more\n
than one parameter from a list of\n
parameters that exclude each other.
193:
Execution error\n
Task %.16s: Late binding\n
procedure call error %i\n
\t\n
There is an error in the procedure call\n
instruction. See previous message for\n
the actual cause.
194:
Value error\n
Task %.16s: Division by zero\n
\t\n
Cannot divide by 0. Rewrite the program\n
so that the divide operation is not\n
executed when the divisor is 0.
195:
Limit error\n
Task %.16s: Exceeded maximum\n
number %i of allowed RETRYs\n
\t\n
The error correction performed before\n
the RETRY instruction is executed, is\n
probably not enough to cure the error.\n
Check the error handler.
196:
Instruction error\n
Task %.16s: Attempt to\n
execute place holder\n
\t\n
Remove the place holder or the\n
instruction containing it, or make the\n
instruction complete. Then continue\n
execution.
197:
Execution error\n
Task %.16s: Function does not\n
return any value\n
\t\n
The end of the function has been reached\n
without a RETURN instruction being\n
executed. Add a RETURN instruction\n
specifying a function return value.
198:
Value error\n
Task %.16s: Illegal\n
orientation value\n
%.40s\n
\t\n
Attempt to use illegal orientation\n
(quaternion) value
199:
Value error\n
Task %.16s: Illegal error\n
number %i in RAISE\n
\t\n
Only error numbers in the range 1-99 are\n
allowed in the RAISE instruction.
200:
Limit error\n
Task %.16s: No more interrupt\n
number available\n
\t\n
There is a limited number of interrupt\n
numbers available. Rewrite the program\n
to use fewer interrupt numbers. This\n
message may also occur as a consequence\n
of a system error.
201:
Value error\n
Task %.16s: Negative operand\n
%i not allowed\n
\t\n
The 'MOD' operator only allows non\n
negative operands. Change the program\n
to make sure that the operator is not\n
applied to negative values.
202:
Type error\n
Task %.16s: Dimensions %i\n
and %i of conformant array\n
dimension number %i are incompatible\n
\t\n
The array is not of the expected size.\n
Array assignment may only be performed\n
on arrays of identical size.
203:
Reference error\n
Task %.16s: Optional\n
parameter %.16s not present\n
\t\n
The value of a non present optional\n
parameter may not be referred. Use the\n
predefined function 'Present' to check\n
the presence of the parameter before\n
using its value.
204:
Value error\n
Task %.16s: Array index %i\n
for dimension number %i out of bounds\n
(1-%i)\n
\t\n
The array index value is non-positive or\n
violates the declared size of the array.
205:
Value error\n
Task %.16s: String too long\n
\t\n
String value exceeds the maximum allowed\n
length. Rewrite the program to use\n
strings of lesser length.
221:
Execution error\n
Task %.16s: Execution aborted\n
\t\n
Execution was aborted due to a fatal\n
error.
222:
Limit error\n
Task %.16s: Execution stack\n
overflow\n
\t\n
The program is too complex to execute.\n
Probably the program contains recursive\n
routines.
223:
Execution error\n
Task %.16s: Fatal runtime\n
error\n
\t\n
A fatal runtime error has occurred.\n
Fatal runtime errors causes immediate\n
termination of execution. See previous\n
message for the actual cause.
224:
Execution error\n
Task %.16s: Illegal return\n
code %i from ReaL routine\n
\t\n
This is always caused by an internal\n
error in the ReaL routine.
225:
Execution error\n
Task %.16s: Execution could\n
not be restarted\n
\t\n
Execution could not be continued after\n
power failure. Restart the program.
226:
Name error\n
Task %.16s: Procedure name\n
%.40s\n
is not a RAPID identifier excluding\n
reserved words\n
\t\n
The procedure name, must be a legal\n
RAPID identifier not equal to any of\n
the reserved words of the RAPID\n
language. Change the name expression.
227:
Limit error\n
Task %.16s: Runtime stack\n
overflow\n
\t\n
The program is too complex to execute.\n
Probably the program contains recursive\n
routines.
228:
Execution error\n
Task %.16s: Unhandled\n
non-fatal runtime error %i\n
\t\n
A non-fatal runtime error has occurred\n
but was not handled by any ERROR\n
clause. See previous message for the\n
actual cause.
229:
Execution error\n
Task %.16s: Unhandled raise\n
error %i\n
\t\n
An error was raised by a RAISE\n
instruction but was not handled by any\n
ERROR clause.
230:
Execution error\n
Task %.16s: Unhandled\n
non-fatal runtime error\n
\t\n
A non-fatal runtime error has occurred\n
but was not handled by any ERROR\n
clause.
241:
Value error\n
Task %.16s: Array dimension\n
number %G out of range (1-%i)\n
\t\n
The value of the 'DimNo' parameter of\n
the 'Dim' function must be an integer\n
value in the specified range.
242:
Type error\n
Task %.16s: Data is not an\n
array\n
\t\n
The 'DatObj' parameter of the 'Dim'\n
function must be an array.
243:
Value error\n
Task %.16s: Unknown interrupt\n
number\n
\t\n
Check that the specified interrupt\n
variable has been initialized by\n
CONNECT, and that the interrupt has been\n
defined using the ISignalDI or other\n
interrupt definition instruction.
251:
Name error\n
Task %.16s: Ambiguous symbol\n
name %.16s\n
\t\n
Installed objects must have names that\n
are unique. Rename the object or change\n
the conflicting name.
252:
Limit error\n
Task %.16s: Error %i when\n
creating sdb entry for %.16s\n
\t\n
An error occurred when the persistent\n
was to be inserted into the shared\n
database. Probably the database is full.
253:
Type definition error\n
Task %.16s: Alias\n
%.16s of alias\n
%.16s not allowed\n
\t\n
Is is not possible to define an alias\n
type equal to another alias type.\n
Instead, define two alias types equal to\n
the same atomic or record type.
254:
Symbol definition error\n
Task %.16s: 'ANYTYPE#'\n
parameter %.16s cannot be\n
dimensioned\n
\t\n
Remove the dimension specification.\n
'ANYTYPE#' includes array types.
255:
Symbol definition error\n
Task %.16s: 'ANYTYPE#' only\n
allowed for parameter (not for\n
%.16s)\n
\t\n
Use another type.
256:
Parameter error\n
Task %.16s: 'alt' must not be\n
set for first optional parameter\n
%.16s in alternatives list\n
\t\n
Make sure that only the second and\n
following in each list of excluding\n
optional parameters are marked as\n
alternatives.
257:
Parameter error\n
Task %.16s: REF mode\n
parameter %.16s cannot be\n
dimensioned\n
\t\n
Remove the array dimension\n
specification, or change the mode of the\n
parameter.
258:
Parameter error\n
Task %.16s: 'switch'\n
parameter %.16s can not be\n
dimensioned\n
\t\n
Remove the array dimension\n
specification, or change the data type\n
of the parameter.
259:
Parameter error\n
Task %.16s: 'switch'\n
parameter %.16s must have\n
transfer mode IN (specified value\n
%i)\n
\t\n
Remove the parameter transfer mode\n
specifier. If IN transfer mode is not\n
sufficient, change the data type of the\n
parameter.
260:
Symbol definition error\n
Task %.16s: 'switch' only\n
allowed for optional parameter\n
(not for %.16s)\n
\t\n
Change the parameter into an optional\n
parameter, or change the data type of\n
the parameter. If the object is not a\n
parameter, change the data type.
261:
Type definition error\n
Task %.16s: Value type class\n
for %.16s must be one of\n
REAL_SYMVALTYP_VAL, _SEMIVAL, _NONVAL or\n
_NONE (specified value %i)\n
\t\n
Change the value type class.
262:
Data declaration error\n
Task %.16s: Too many array\n
dimensions for %.16s\n
(specified value %i)\n
\t\n
An array may have at most 3 dimensions.
263:
Name error\n
Task %.16s: Symbol name\n
%.40s\n
is not a RAPID identifier excluding\n
reserved words\n
\t\n
The names of installed objects,\n
including parameters and components,\n
must be legal RAPID identifiers not\n
equal to any of the reserved words of\n
the RAPID language. Change the name.
264:
Symbol definition error\n
Task %.16s: Missing C\n
function for %.16s\n
\t\n
A C-function that executes the ReaL\n
function being defined, must be\n
specified.
265:
Symbol definition error\n
Task %.16s: Missing value\n
initialization function for\n
%.16s\n
\t\n
A value initialization function must be\n
specified.
266:
Reference error\n
Task %.16s: %.16s\n
is not a data type name (object\n
%.16s)\n
\t\n
The specified name identifies an object\n
other than a type.
267:
Reference error\n
Task %.16s: %.16s\n
is not a value data type (object\n
%.16s)\n
\t\n
Only record components, alias types,\n
variables and 'VAR' mode parameters may\n
be of semi-value or non-value type.
268:
Symbol definition error\n
Task %.16s: Missing value\n
conversion function for %.16s\n
\t\n
A value conversion function must be\n
specified for a semi-value type.
269:
Symbol definition error\n
Task %.16s: Not enough\n
memory for value of data\n
%.16s\n
\t\n
More memory required.
270:
Type definition error\n
Task %.16s: Private type\n
%.16s can only be semi-value\n
or non-value type (specified value\n
%i)\n
\t\n
Change the value type class.
271:
Type definition error\n
Task %.16s: Private type\n
%.16s size must be multiple\n
of 4 (specified value %i)\n
\t\n
All RAPID types must have a size that is\n
a multiple of four. Change the specified\n
type size.
272:
Type error\n
Task %.16s: Persistent type\n
mismatch for %.16s\n
\t\n
There is already a persistent data with\n
the same name but with another data\n
type. Rename the persistent, or change\n
its data type.
273:
Reference error\n
Task %.16s: Unknown data type\n
name %.16s for\n
%.16s\n
\t\n
There is no data type (or other object)\n
with the specified name.
274:
Parameter error\n
Task %.16s: Unknown parameter\n
transfer mode %i for\n
%.16s\n
\t\n
The specified parameter transfer mode is\n
not one of IN, 'VAR', 'PERS', 'INOUT' or\n
REF. Use corresponding REAL_SYMPARMOD_x.
275:
Symbol definition error\n
Task %.16s: Unknown symbol\n
definition type %i\n
\t\n
The symbol definition type tag does not\n
specifiy one of the allowed symbol\n
types (REAL_SYMDEF_x).
301:
I/O error\n
Task %.16s: Permission denied\n
(file name\n
%.39s)\n
\t\n

302:
I/O error\n
Task %.16s: No such file or\n
directory (file name\n
%.39s)\n
\t\n

303:
I/O error\n
Task %.16s: No space left on\n
device (file name\n
%.39s)\n
\t\n

304:
I/O error\n
Task %.16s: I/O error %!\n
(file name\n
%.39s)\n
\t\n
One of:\n
 Permission denied (write protected)\n
 No such file or directory\n
 No space left on device
321:
Load error\n
Task %.16s:\n
Module loaded with path\n
%.40s\n
is active\n
\t\n
A module containing routines or data\n
that are still active cannot be erased.\n

322:
Load error\n
Task %.16s: RAPID syntax\n
error(s) in file\n
%.40s\n
\t\n
The source file to be loaded contains\n
RAPID syntax errors. Correct the source\n
file. The syntax errors are logged in a\n
separate file.
323:
Load error\n
Task %.16s: Syntax error(s)\n
in header in file\n
%.40s\n
\t\n
The source file to be loaded contains\n
syntax error in the file header. Correct\n
the source file. The syntax errors are\n
logged in a separate file.
324:
Load error\n
Task %.16s: Keywords not\n
defined in specified language (file\n
%.39s)\n
\t\n
Cannot load RAPID source code in the\n
national language specified in the file\n
header.
325:
Load error\n
Task %.16s: Not enough heap\n
space\n
\t\n
There is not enough free memory left.
326:
Load error\n
Task %.16s: Parser stack\n
full (file\n
%.39s)\n
\t\n
The program is too complex to load.
327:
Load error\n
Task %.16s: Not current RAPID\n
version (file\n
%.39s)\n
\t\n
Cannot load RAPID source code of the\n
version specified in the file header.
351:
Memory allocation error\n
Task %.16s: Failed to\n
allocate hash table, use linear list\n
\t\n

352:
Memory allocation error\n
Task %.16s: Failed to\n
update persistent expression, keep old\n
one\n
\t\n

501:
Timeout\n
%s\n
%s\n
\t\n

502:
Digital input break\n
%s\n
%s\n
\t\n

503:
Reference error\n
Device descriptor is\n
%s\n
\t\n

504:
Parameter error\n
%s\n
\t\n
%s
505:
File access error\n
%s\n
\t\n

506:
System access error\n
%s\n
\t\n

507:
Limit error\n
%s\n
\t\n

508:
Wrong orientation value\n
in %s\n
\t\n

509:
Search warning\n
%s\n
Before performing next search,\n
make sure that TCP is moved back\n
to the start position of the\n
search path.\n
\t\n
If no repositioning is done, before\n
restart of circular search, movement\n
that can cause damage might occur.
510:
Security warning\n
The move instruction can't restart\n
due to security problem.\n
Try to move the PP\n
\t\n

511:
Parameter error\n
The parameter %s in %s\n
is specified as a negative value\n
\t\n
The parameter must be set positive.
512:
Missing ext. axis value\n
Some active external axis have\n
incorrect or no order value.\n
Reprogram the position.\n
\t\n

513:
Mechanical unit error\n
Not possible to activate or\n
deactivate mechanical unit.\n
\t\n

514:
Execution error\n
Too far from path to perform\n
StartMove of the interrupted\n
movement.\n
\t\n
Position the robot to the\n
interrupted position in the program.
515:
Type error\n
Task %s: Illegal data type\n
of argument for parameter %s\n
\t\n

516:
Value error\n
Task %s: Illegal value of\n
argument for parameter %s\n
\t\n

517:
Search error\n
%s\n
No search hit or more than 1 search\n
hit during stepwise forward execution.\n
The search instruction is ready and\n
next instruction can be executed.\n
\t\n
Note that no position has been returned\n
from the search instruction.
518:
Type error\n
%s\n
\t\n

600:
Argument error\n
No \WObj specified\n
for movement with stationary TCP.\n
\t\n
Add argument \WObj\n
for actual work object.\n
If not movement with stationary TCP,\n
change argument Tool to\n
"robot holds the tool".
601:
Argument error\n
Undefined if robot holds the tool\n
or the work object.\n
\t\n
Check if mismatch between\n
argument Tool and argument \WObj\n
for data component robhold.
602:
Argument error\n
Argument %s has\n
at least one data component\n
with negative value.\n
\t\n
Set all data components\n
in argument %s\n
to positive values.
603:
Argument error\n
Argument %s has a\n
not allowed negative value.\n
\t\n
Set argument %s to positive.
604:
Argument error\n
Argument Tool has undefined\n
load of the tool.\n
\t\n
Define the actual load of the tool\n
before use of the tool for jogging\n
or program movement.
605:
Argument error\n
Argument Tool has negative\n
load of the tool.\n
\t\n
Define the correct load of the tool\n
before use of the tool for jogging\n
or program movement.
606:
Argument error\n
Argument Tool has at least\n
one inertia data component\n
with negative value.\n
\t\n
Define all inertia data components\n
(ix, iy or iz) to actual\n
positive values.
607:
Execution error\n
Not allowed to change run mode\n
from forward to backward or vice versa\n
during running a circular movement.\n
\t\n
If possible, select the original run\n
mode and press start to continue the\n
stopped circular movement. If not\n
possible, move robot and program pointer\n
for a new start.
608:
Argument error\n
Orientation definition error\n
in %s.\n
\t\n
All used orientations must be normalized\n
i.e. the sum of the quaternion elements\n
squares must equal 1.
609:
Argument error\n
Argument \WObj specifies a mechanical\n
unit with too long name.\n
\t\n
Use max. 16 characters to specify the\n
name of a mechanical coordinated unit.
610:
Argument error\n
Argument \WObj specifies a mechanical\n
unit name, which is not activated\n
or unknown in the system.\n
\t\n
The mechanical unit name defined in\n
\WObj must correspond to the name\n
earlier defined in the system\n
parameters and must be activated.
611:
Execution error\n
Not allowed to step backwards\n
with actual instruction.\n
\t\n
Not allowed to step backwards in\n
a circular movement, if the endpoint\n
of the circular movement is defined\n
with another tool and/or work object.
612:
Argument error\n
No argument programmed for\n
the name of the output signal.\n
\t\n
Possible to set one position fix IO\n
such as digital, group of digitals\n
or analog output signal during the\n
robot movement.
613:
Argument error\n
Optional argument %s\n
can only be combined with\n
output signal argument %s.\n
\t\n

614:
Argument error\n
Argument %s\n
is not 0 or 1.\n
\t\n
Digital output signals can only be\n
set to 0 or 1.
615:
Argument error\n
Argument %s\n
is not an integer value.\n
\t\n
Digital group of output signals\n
can only have an integer value.
616:
Argument error\n
Argument %s\n
is outside allowed limits.\n
\t\n
Used group of digital output signals\n
can only be set within 0 to %s\n
according configuration in\n
the system parameters.
617:
Argument error\n
Argument %s\n
is outside allowed limits.\n
\t\n
Used analog output signals can only be\n
set within %s to %s\n
according configuration in\n
the system parameters.
618:
Argument error\n
Argument %s\n
contains an illegal interrupt number.\n
\t\n
Input interrupt number is illegal\n
because it has not been allocated by\n
the instruction CONNECT.\n
CONNECT do allocation and connection of\n
interrupt number to trap routine.
619:
Argument error\n
Argument %s\n
contains an interrupt number, which\n
is already in use for other purpose.\n
\t\n
Before reuse of an interrupt variable\n
again in the program, cancel old\n
interrupt generation and interrupt\n
number with instruction IDelete.
622:
Argument error\n
The value of argument Time in ITimer\n
is too low for cyclic interrupts.\n
\t\n

623:
Argument error\n
The value of argument Time in ITimer\n
is too low for single interrupts.\n
\t\n

631:
Instruction error\n
Too many move instructions in\n
sequence with concurrent RAPID\n
program execution.\n
\t\n
Edit the program to max. 5 MoveX \Conc\n
in sequence on the basic\n
execution level of the program.
632:
Instruction error\n
No move instructions with concurrent\n
RAPID program execution are allowed\n
within the StorePath-RestoPath part of\n
the program.\n
\t\n
Edit the program so it does not\n
contain any MoveX \Conc instructions\n
within the StorePath-RestoPath part of\n
the program.
633:
Reference error\n
Trigg parameter no %s reference to\n
undefined trigg data.\n
\t\n
Define trigg data by executing\n
instruction TriggIO, TriggInt,\n
TriggEquip or TriggSpeed\n
before TriggL, TriggC or TriggJ.
634:
Reference error\n
Signal reference in parameter %s\n
contains unknown signal for the robot.\n
\t\n
All signals should be defined in the\n
system parameters and should not be\n
defined in the RAPID program.
635:
Reference error\n
Argument reference in parameter %s\n
is not a entire persistent variable.\n
\t\n
Not possible to use record component or\n
array element in argument %s.\n
Only possible to use entire persistent\n
variables for Tool, WObj or Load\n
in any motion instructions.
636:
Sensor error\n
No measurement from sensor.\n
\t\n
Requested data is not available.
637:
Sensor error\n
Not ready yet.\n
\t\n
Requested function is not ready yet.
638:
Sensor error\n
General error.\n
\t\n
General error has occurred which is not\n
specifically connected to the requested\n
action. Read the block "Error log" if\n
the function is available.
639:
Sensor error\n
Sensor busy, try later.\n
\t\n
The sensor is busy with an other\n
function.
640:
Sensor error\n
Unknown command.\n
\t\n
The function requested from the sensor\n
is unknown.
641:
Sensor error\n
Illegal variable or block number.\n
\t\n
Requested variable or block is not\n
defined in the sensor.
642:
Sensor error\n
External alarm.\n
\t\n
Alarm from external equipment.
643:
Sensor error\n
Camera alarm.\n
\t\n
Some error has been detected in\n
the camera. Run Camcheck to test if the\n
camera is OK.
644:
Sensor error\n
Temperature alarm.\n
\t\n
The camera is overheated it needs more\n
cooling air or water.
645:
Sensor error\n
Value out of range.\n
\t\n
The value of the data sent to the\n
sensor is out of range.
646:
Sensor error\n
Camera check failed.\n
\t\n
The CAMCHECK function failed. The\n
camera is broken. Send it for repair.
647:
Sensor error\n
Communication time out.\n
\t\n
Increase the time out time and check\n
the connections to the sensor.
648:
Search error\n
Not possible to do StorePath while\n
searching on basic path level.\n
\t\n
If using program with robot movement\n
in TRAP, then such interrupt must be\n
deactivated during any searching.\n
E.g. ISleep - SearchL - IWatch
649:
Path limit error\n
%s already done.\n
\t\n
Instruction %s must first be\n
executed, before a new %s can\n
be done.
650:
Wrong param combination\n
Optional parameters and switches are\n
not used in a correct combination.\n
\t\n
No optional parameters and no switch\n
keeps the old coordinate system.\n
The switch Old has the same function.\n
RefPos or RefNum has to be defined with\n
Short, Fwd or Bwd.
651:
Use numeric input\n
Use numeric input for the position\n
instead of a robtarget.\n
\t\n
The position can not be defined with\n
a robtarget for robot axes.\n
Use the optional parameter for numeric\n
input of the position.
652:
Axis is moving\n
A Robot axis, an external axis\n
or an independent axis is moving.\n
\t\n
All Robot axes, external axes and\n
independent axes have to stand still.\n
E.g Use MoveL with Fine argument for\n
the Robot and ext. axes. And IndRMove\n
for the independent axes.
653:
Switch is missing\n
One of the switch parameters %s\n
or %s has to be defined.\n
\t\n

654:
Axis is not active\n
The axis is not active\n
or it is not defined.\n
\t\n
The mechanical unit has to be activated\n
and the axis has to be defined, before\n
this instruction is executed and before\n
a robtarget is saved.
655:
Axis is not independent\n
The axis is not in independent mode.\n
\t\n
It is only possible to get the status\n
from an axis in independent mode.
656:
Execution error\n
Not possible to set a new scale\n
value for the AO signal.\n
\t\n
The internal process, that control\n
the output of the AO signal,\n
are for some unknown reason "dead".
657:
Execution error\n
The output of the AO signal are not\n
TCP-speed proportional any more.\n
The reason could be following:\n
\t\n
- Use of MoveX between TriggX instr.\n
- No setup of TriggSpeed in the used\n
  TriggX instr.\n
- The used ScaleLag is too small in\n
  relation to actual robot servo lag.
658:
Parameter error\n
Parameter %s can only\n
be used, if parameter %s\n
is greater than zero.\n
\t\n
Parameter %s has effect\n
only in the first TriggX, in a\n
sequence of several TriggX, that\n
controls the speed proportional\n
AO signal.
659:
Undefined load\n
WARNING: Argument %.16s has\n
undefined load (mass equal to 0 kg).\n
IMPORTANT TO DEFINE CORRECT LOAD to\n
avoid mechanical damages of the robot\n
and to get good motion performance.\n
\t\n
Define the actual load for the tool\n
or the grip load before program\n
movement or jogging.
660:
Undefined load\n
WARNING: Argument %.16s has\n
undefined load centre of gravity.\n
IMPORTANT TO DEFINE CORRECT LOAD to\n
avoid mechanical damages of the robot\n
and to get good motion performance.\n
\t\n
Define the actual centre of gravity\n
for the tool load or the grip load\n
before program movement or jogging\n
(cog.x, cog.y and cog.z can not be\n
0 mm at the same time).
670:
Execution error\n
Not allowed to change run mode\n
from forward to backward or vice versa\n
during running a invisible trap routine.\n
\t\n
If possible, select the original run\n
mode and press start to continue the\n
If not possible, move robot and\n
program pointer for a new start.
700:
Syntax error\n
Task %s: Syntax error\n
%s\n
\t\n

701:
Program memory full\n
The task %s, has only\n
%i free bytes in its\n
user space\n
\t\n
Remove some other module and try\n
again.
702:
File not found\n
%.40s\n
The file path or the file name is wrong\n
or the file doesn't exist.
703:
Load error\n
%.40s\n
The program module couldn't be loaded.\n
\t\n
The program module have some errors.
704:
UnLoad error\n
%.40s\n
The program module couldn't be unloaded\n
The reason could be:\n
- Module not loaded with Load instr.\n
- Not same file path as used for Load\n
\t\n
The program module must have been\n
loaded with the instruction Load.\n
The file path and name must be the same\n
in the UnLoad and Load instruction.
705:
Syntax error\n
Task %s: Syntax error\n
%s\n
\t\n
More syntax errors will follow this
706:
Loaded error\n
The program module is already loaded\n
\t\n
The module name in the head of the file\n
%.40s\n
already exists in the program memory

712:
Event routine error\n
Task %s: Too many event routines\n
the routine %s, will not be\n
executed. Maximum 4 routines\n
could be specified for each event\n
\t\n
Encapslulate the routine in one of\n
the others that are specified for\n
the save event.

